it was the deadliest european conflict  since the second world war  a hundred thousand people killed more  than two million displaced  nationalism that led to ethnic cleansing  sexual violence and genocide  a brutal conflict that tore bosnia apart  the bosnian war ended nearly three  decades ago but many of the underlying  issues that led to it are now  re-emerging the solution of  bosnia-herzegovina will happen  if the situation in this state continues  like this so could we really see another  war break out here  [Music]  the story of bosnia is complex but it's  vital to our understanding of what's  happening here today  bosnia is made up of three main ethnic  groups bosniaks who are mostly muslims  bosnian serbs  and bosnian croats  now an important thing to remember is  that these groups are separate from  serbs in serbia or croats in croatia but  they do have close ties and a shared  history  after the breakup of yugoslavia in the  early 1990s nationalism started to rise  in the now independent nations this led  to issues in each of those states as  well as wars in croatia and slovenia but  bosnia was particularly complicated due  to its multi-ethnic makeup many of the  bosnian serbs wanted to be part of  serbia  many of the bosnian croats  wanted to be part of croatia  the bosnian serbs were led by this man  radovan karadic  he threatened violence if bosnia were to  become independent  and then in early 1992  the un  did recognize bosnia and herzegovina as  an independent nation after a referendum  and fighting julie broke out  it was  a difficult complicated conflict with  all sides battling each other but the  bulk of the conflict was between the  serbs and the bosniaks  it was a particularly horrible war  bitter brutal and bloody with  mass rapes massacres and the first use  of the term ethnic cleansing the bosnian  serbs backed by the serbian president  slobodan milosevic took over huge parts  of bosnia  they forced tens of thousands of bosniak  muslims and croats out of their homes  in mass ethnic cleansing  thousands and thousands died  mostly bosniaks  the capital of bosnia sarajevo was  bombarded daily in one of the longest  military sieges in modern history and  here in srebrenica bosnian serb forces  under the commander raccoon lavish  [Music]  8 000 bosniak men and boys it was the  worst massacre in europe since the  holocaust  shahida's husband and brother were both  killed her teenage son escaped death by  fleeing into the woods  foreign  the war finally came to an end in 1995  with the dayton accords  [Applause]  dividing up bosnia into two autonomous  regions the federation of bosnia and  herzegovina  and the serb controlled republica  serpska  dayton implemented a complicated  political system in the country to  introduce power sharing it relies on  nations such as the us and the european  union to maintain the peace  bosnia even has a three-pronged  presidency that rotates every eight  months between a serb a croat and a  bosniak  the dayton accords did bring a shaky  peace to bosnia but after three and a  half years of bloodshed people here were  divided and traumatized  now some of those old wounds are  beginning to open 30 years later bosnia  is once again caught in a complex  volatile dispute that embraces ethnic  divisions and religious rivalry  [Music]  [Applause]  and this man miller dodec is at the  heart of it  he is the serb member of bosnia's  rotating three-pronged presidency he's  also a former prime minister and former  president of republika srpska  he's an ultra-nationalist he's  threatened to take republica serpska out  of bosnia and herzegovina he also  downplays the genocide at srebrenica and  speaks of his admiration for the russian  president vladimir putin  the  sort of  nationalist views that he has are more  or less common amongst all serb  political parties all major serve  political parties he does not  uh in any way think that compromise is  possible uh and in a country a  multinational country such as boston  herzegovina compromise is the key aspect  to moving forward in any sort of policy  direction  if this is not a possibility as  miloradox says then the only other  possibility is for the country to break  up which is clearly on his agenda and  donick is not some curious outlier in  fact many share his views  the butcher of bosnia is a hero to many  and nationalists like dodic and pavlovic  want to see republika serpska run its  own affairs and raise its own army they  also claim serbs in bosnia are being  marginalized by the bosniaks  talk like this all seems depressingly  familiar  but a new aspect to this equation is the  role of russia  we have a very very strong historical  relations with the russians  and we believe in russians  personally i love russians  more than americans putin is admired by  nationalists here he and doric have a  strong relationship  russia is also close to serbia an  obvious ally to the bosnian serbs who  are just over the border and many in the  west fear russia is trying to find new  ways to undermine european unity  from a russian perspective another  european war  might distract from ukraine stretch nato  and challenge the status quo  and bosnia with all its divisions and  its volatility  could be the perfect candidate  so could this actually escalate into  another war  it all depends on how much presence the  international community is willing to  have in the country  throughout the past 10 years or so  there's been very little  investments from the european union from  the united states from nato from  basically any international actor  in trying to  keep bosnian politics in line and we  have seen this escalation as a result of  this  so what you do have is a country that  does not really want  war as such but wants to resolve  conflict in other ways  and this ability to resolve conflict in  other ways it's not there and you're  you're stuck in a way that  you want to move forward but not with  the current leaders because they're not  willing to do so  sarajevo is now not too different from  other modern european capitals far  from the war-torn besieged city of the  1990s  and there does seem to be little desire  for another war across both regions of  bosnia a 2021 survey found that 45  percent of residents of republica serbs  are actually against independence from  bosnia 35  say they support that idea  72 percent of the people responding said  that avoiding inter-ethnic violence was  the most pressing issue in the country  but some who lived through the previous  war  do fear a return  [Music]  [Music]  the dayton accords created a piece that  has lasted a long time  but bosnia remains a divided and hurt  country  and the trauma that many hoped to leave  in the past could soon be brought  kicking and screaming  into the present day
 

